+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more number and unit groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), and `s` (seconds), like `2d`, `1h30m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. Each unit can be used at most once and in that
+order, with no spaces. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed
+as a default or from Python is used unchanged.
+
+Negative values, decimals like `1.5h`, unknown units, and repeated or out-of-order units are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
